{"id": "pa-00087", "model": "minicpm5", "cell": "18", "catalog_size": 18, "sampling": "greedy", "machine": "gpu", "server": {"model_path": "/content/models/MiniCPM5-2B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "build": "b12555-c406f4367"}, "calls": [], "content": "Here's some practical advice for sharp, glare-free photos of flat paper documents:\n\n1. **Natural Light**  \n   Shoot during the day with indirect, even lighting (avoid backlighting). Direct sunlight can create glare; diffuse it with a soft box or white wall.\n\n2. **Flat Surface**  \n   Place the document on a non-reflective surface like a white tablecloth or mat. Avoid glossy surfaces that reflect light.\n\n3. **Close Proximity**  \n   Use a close-up macro setting to keep the paper flat and eliminate perspective distortion.\n\n4. **Clean Paper**  \n   Ensure the document is clean and free of dust or smudges, which can look like glare.\n\n5. **Fill the Frame**  \n   Use the full frame to avoid cropping edges that might introduce shadows.\n\n6. **Aperture**  \n   Use a narrow aperture (small f-number) to maximize depth of field and keep the document sharp.\n\n7. **Focus**  \n   Focus precisely on the text lines to avoid blur from out-of-focus areas.\n\n8. **Clean Lens**  \n   A dirty lens adds glare; wipe it before shooting.\n\nWould you like me to search for more specific tips or resources on this?", "format_error": false, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 2097, "completion_tokens": 255, "cached_tokens": 0, "prompt_ms": 932.539, "decode_ms": 3529.365, "decode_tok_per_s": 71.96762023763482, "wall_ms": 4562.152954999874}
